00:41:30.420 --> 00:41:32.040
Abhishek, would you mind
introducing yourself?

00:41:32.040 --> 00:41:33.150
ABHISHEK KASHYAP:
Yeah, thank you, Nick.

00:41:33.150 --> 00:41:35.080
Hi, everyone, I'm
Abhishek Kashyap.

00:41:35.080 --> 00:41:38.610
And I'm the product manager
and really delighted

00:41:38.610 --> 00:41:42.406
to be here and talk to
you about BigQuery ML.

00:41:42.406 --> 00:41:43.280
NICK ORLOVE: Awesome.

00:41:43.280 --> 00:41:46.260
Yeah, so big data, machine
learning-- for any of you

00:41:46.260 --> 00:41:48.322
out there who are
playing buzzword bingo,

00:41:48.322 --> 00:41:50.280
now's your time to bring
out those scratchcards

00:41:50.280 --> 00:41:52.920
because I think that we'll
be highlighting some pretty

00:41:52.920 --> 00:41:55.320
exciting stuff going through.

00:41:55.320 --> 00:41:57.870
So in order to start
talking about BigQuery ML,

00:41:57.870 --> 00:42:01.830
I think we should at least
first cover what BigQuery is.

00:42:01.830 --> 00:42:04.500
So for any of you who are
unaware of what BigQuery is,

00:42:04.500 --> 00:42:07.410
BigQuery is our fully manged
enterprise data warehouse

00:42:07.410 --> 00:42:10.950
solution that essentially
provides enterprise features

00:42:10.950 --> 00:42:13.590
and functionality for
those real-time SQL

00:42:13.590 --> 00:42:15.750
queries that you need to run.

00:42:15.750 --> 00:42:17.490
It's pretty unique
because it has

00:42:17.490 --> 00:42:20.430
Google's unique backend
architecture of separating

00:42:20.430 --> 00:42:23.460
compute and storage so
that, as you scale up

00:42:23.460 --> 00:42:25.830
your compute environment,
as you scale up your storage

00:42:25.830 --> 00:42:27.450
environment,
basically, everything

00:42:27.450 --> 00:42:29.040
is completely seamless to you.

00:42:29.040 --> 00:42:32.220
All you see as the end user is,
you upload data to BigQuery,

00:42:32.220 --> 00:42:34.440
you hit the giant big red
button that says Run Query,

00:42:34.440 --> 00:42:35.669
and you get some results.

00:42:35.669 --> 00:42:37.710
And the other amazing
thing is that, because it's

00:42:37.710 --> 00:42:40.050
fully managed, Google takes
care of your encryption,

00:42:40.050 --> 00:42:43.560
takes care of the reliability,
availability, all that stuff,

00:42:43.560 --> 00:42:48.076
all that typical heartache
and heavy weight with managing

00:42:48.076 --> 00:42:49.450
clusters, all that
kind of stuff.

00:42:49.450 --> 00:42:51.900
So we take that burden
off of our users.

00:42:51.900 --> 00:42:53.400
So Abhishek, since
we're going to be

00:42:53.400 --> 00:42:59.190
talking about BigQuery ML, why
are those two topics related?

00:42:59.190 --> 00:43:01.390
Why big data plus
machine learning?

00:43:01.390 --> 00:43:03.570
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: There
are two topics related

00:43:03.570 --> 00:43:07.680
because, if you think about
where the world industry is

00:43:07.680 --> 00:43:12.660
going, there used be a lot of
historical analysis of data.

00:43:12.660 --> 00:43:16.310
If you look at the journey of
analytics on data in the data

00:43:16.310 --> 00:43:21.060
warehouse, everyone use to
clean their data processor

00:43:21.060 --> 00:43:24.504
to build dashboards and
present to executives

00:43:24.504 --> 00:43:25.920
and make decisions
based on those.

00:43:25.920 --> 00:43:26.175
NICK ORLOVE: Sure.

00:43:26.175 --> 00:43:26.430
Yeah, that makes sense.

00:43:26.430 --> 00:43:28.230
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: But
all these decisions

00:43:28.230 --> 00:43:31.010
were based on historical trends.

00:43:31.010 --> 00:43:33.450
As we see the advent of
machine learning and machine

00:43:33.450 --> 00:43:37.510
learning at the scale to
work on terabytes of data,

00:43:37.510 --> 00:43:41.110
all these decisions could
be made in a predictive way.

00:43:41.110 --> 00:43:43.650
So rather than using
somebody's wisdom

00:43:43.650 --> 00:43:45.404
on what's going to
happen tomorrow,

00:43:45.404 --> 00:43:47.070
you could actually
have machine learning

00:43:47.070 --> 00:43:49.860
apply to every decision
that gets taken.

00:43:49.860 --> 00:43:53.070
And the way we see this
manifesting is, just

00:43:53.070 --> 00:43:57.720
like BigQuery made it
very, very easy to go

00:43:57.720 --> 00:44:00.990
from having to manage your
clusters, and the node size

00:44:00.990 --> 00:44:05.400
and the performance to only
thinking about analysis

00:44:05.400 --> 00:44:08.650
and writing SQL to
do the analysis,

00:44:08.650 --> 00:44:11.250
we believe machine learning
should be the same way.

00:44:11.250 --> 00:44:12.780
You should not
have to think about

00:44:12.780 --> 00:44:15.040
the underlying infrastructure.

00:44:15.040 --> 00:44:18.060
How many nodes do
I need to provision

00:44:18.060 --> 00:44:19.520
to build a machine
learning model?

00:44:19.520 --> 00:44:23.530
Or do I need to split my
data into cleaning and test?

00:44:23.530 --> 00:44:25.300
Yes, you always should.

00:44:25.300 --> 00:44:27.540
So what if we
automated all of these

00:44:27.540 --> 00:44:29.850
and build the machine
learning foundations

00:44:29.850 --> 00:44:33.145
where their data is, which,
in our case, is BigQuery?

00:44:33.145 --> 00:44:34.020
NICK ORLOVE: Awesome.

00:44:34.020 --> 00:44:35.186
Yeah, that's exciting stuff.

00:44:38.270 --> 00:44:39.830
Yeah, so I'm a
little bit curious.

00:44:39.830 --> 00:44:43.820
How have customers previously
used BigQuery plus machine

00:44:43.820 --> 00:44:45.150
learning in the past?

00:44:45.150 --> 00:44:50.420
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: So if you
think about a typical process,

00:44:50.420 --> 00:44:54.950
since it's really
hard to do machine

00:44:54.950 --> 00:44:57.590
learning where the
data is, the first step

00:44:57.590 --> 00:45:03.320
is people end up exporting
data to an object store.

00:45:03.320 --> 00:45:07.020
And from there, if you
are a data analyst,

00:45:07.020 --> 00:45:13.380
you would take a small sample to
Sheets, do a linear regression.

00:45:13.380 --> 00:45:15.710
You have an idea to
improve the model.

00:45:15.710 --> 00:45:19.350
You go back, export more data,
and do it all over again.

00:45:19.350 --> 00:45:22.230
If you happen to be
a data scientist,

00:45:22.230 --> 00:45:26.395
you can take your
favorite Python framework

00:45:26.395 --> 00:45:30.740
and convert the data into frames
that that framework takes,

00:45:30.740 --> 00:45:31.900
build your model.

00:45:31.900 --> 00:45:35.260
To improve, you go
back and get more data.

00:45:35.260 --> 00:45:37.200
That's the process
that you follow.

00:45:37.200 --> 00:45:42.000
And it takes hours and days
for each iteration just because

00:45:42.000 --> 00:45:43.650
of the backend
work that's needed.

00:45:43.650 --> 00:45:44.400
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah.

00:45:44.400 --> 00:45:46.472
I can see an issue
just-- not only for time,

00:45:46.472 --> 00:45:48.180
because it takes time
to obviously export

00:45:48.180 --> 00:45:50.880
the data from BigQuery,
import into GCS,

00:45:50.880 --> 00:45:53.340
or whatever your framework
is, but also, you're

00:45:53.340 --> 00:45:57.450
starting kind of a bad trend
of creating data silos.

00:45:57.450 --> 00:46:00.030
You have multiple data sources
that you have to manage.

00:46:00.030 --> 00:46:03.040
If you're updating your
BigQuery dataset in real time,

00:46:03.040 --> 00:46:05.740
now you have to somehow get
those over to the GCS buckets.

00:46:05.740 --> 00:46:08.540
So I can definitely see
that as a big challenge.

00:46:08.540 --> 00:46:11.514
How are we how we kind of
running into those issues,

00:46:11.514 --> 00:46:12.930
and how are we
dealing with those?

00:46:12.930 --> 00:46:14.805
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: And
actually to this point,

00:46:14.805 --> 00:46:18.120
it's actually OK to
have silos in GCS.

00:46:18.120 --> 00:46:20.940
We have seen data scientists
having silos on their laptops.

00:46:20.940 --> 00:46:21.660
NICK ORLOVE: OK.

00:46:21.660 --> 00:46:22.160
Good point.

00:46:22.160 --> 00:46:24.140
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: People will
build models on their laptops.

00:46:24.140 --> 00:46:24.810
NICK ORLOVE: Fair point.

00:46:24.810 --> 00:46:27.590
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: And then they
share with each other using

00:46:27.590 --> 00:46:29.820
email and SEP.

00:46:29.820 --> 00:46:34.116
So that's the extent of the
silos that are being created

00:46:34.116 --> 00:46:35.240
with the current processes.

00:46:35.240 --> 00:46:38.000
NICK ORLOVE: OK.

00:46:38.000 --> 00:46:39.909
And so these challenges--

00:46:39.909 --> 00:46:41.700
how are they typically
affecting companies?

00:46:41.700 --> 00:46:42.658
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Yeah.

00:46:42.658 --> 00:46:46.630
So the two challenges
we see are, one--

00:46:46.630 --> 00:46:50.840
most companies do not hire
enough data scientists.

00:46:50.840 --> 00:46:54.230
And it's not that
they do not want to.

00:46:54.230 --> 00:46:56.740
If you just do a simple
search on LinkedIn,

00:46:56.740 --> 00:46:58.950
you'll find that there are
maybe up to 1 million data

00:46:58.950 --> 00:47:00.280
scientists.

00:47:00.280 --> 00:47:02.740
And if you think, how
many of those million

00:47:02.740 --> 00:47:05.820
can actually go ahead and
write your best-in-class neural

00:47:05.820 --> 00:47:08.521
network, that number is
probably 1/10th of that.

00:47:08.521 --> 00:47:09.270
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah.

00:47:09.270 --> 00:47:09.670
Makes sense.

00:47:09.670 --> 00:47:11.503
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: And
if you start thinking,

00:47:11.503 --> 00:47:13.570
well, I need to do
reinforcement learning, that's

00:47:13.570 --> 00:47:15.800
probably thousands of people.

00:47:15.800 --> 00:47:18.850
So the first problem is, if you
are going to have so few data

00:47:18.850 --> 00:47:22.870
scientists, there is no way that
you can apply machine learning

00:47:22.870 --> 00:47:26.260
to more than 2%, 3%, 4%
of the business problems

00:47:26.260 --> 00:47:28.060
that you deal with.

00:47:28.060 --> 00:47:32.980
The second problem is, as
I walk through the process,

00:47:32.980 --> 00:47:37.360
it takes a good six to eight
hours in just setting up

00:47:37.360 --> 00:47:39.905
the infrastructure, even if
you know what you are doing,

00:47:39.905 --> 00:47:42.130
to build your first model.

00:47:42.130 --> 00:47:44.252
So iterations actually
take a lot of time

00:47:44.252 --> 00:47:46.210
because you have to set
up [INAUDIBLE],, set up

00:47:46.210 --> 00:47:47.620
data transfer.

00:47:47.620 --> 00:47:50.320
You have to set up conversion
of data from one format

00:47:50.320 --> 00:47:51.310
to another.

00:47:51.310 --> 00:47:57.010
And that's actually a big,
big productivity inhibitor.

00:47:57.010 --> 00:47:57.810
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah.

00:47:57.810 --> 00:48:00.780
That makes sense.

00:48:00.780 --> 00:48:03.170
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: And that's
what we are trying to fix.

00:48:03.170 --> 00:48:06.010
So we launched
BigQuery ML in beta

00:48:06.010 --> 00:48:09.880
in July at our conference
in San Francisco.

00:48:09.880 --> 00:48:16.420
And it allows anyone who knows
SQL to do machine learning just

00:48:16.420 --> 00:48:19.540
using SQL without having
to learn Python or R

00:48:19.540 --> 00:48:20.710
and within BigQuery.

00:48:20.710 --> 00:48:23.690
So you do not have to
move the data out either.

00:48:23.690 --> 00:48:25.740
And that's what BigQuery ML is.

00:48:25.740 --> 00:48:31.720
It is simply Machine Learning
using SQL in BigQuery.

00:48:31.720 --> 00:48:36.610
And it actually makes ML
accessible for all audiences.

00:48:36.610 --> 00:48:37.360
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah.

00:48:37.360 --> 00:48:38.600
That's a good point.

00:48:38.600 --> 00:48:42.490
So in Google Cloud, we
have different paradigms

00:48:42.490 --> 00:48:43.340
of machine learning.

00:48:43.340 --> 00:48:46.020
So on one side of the
spectrum, you have the ML APIs;

00:48:46.020 --> 00:48:47.104
you have AutoML.

00:48:47.104 --> 00:48:48.520
On the other side
of the spectrum,

00:48:48.520 --> 00:48:50.603
you have full customization
with the cloud machine

00:48:50.603 --> 00:48:51.420
learning engine.

00:48:51.420 --> 00:48:54.070
And so it seems like this
is a good middle ground

00:48:54.070 --> 00:48:56.366
because machine
learning is incredibly

00:48:56.366 --> 00:48:58.240
transformational across
all of our customers.

00:48:58.240 --> 00:49:02.030
I mean, I've seen and heard
some really incredible stories.

00:49:02.030 --> 00:49:05.680
And so I feel like what we're
doing as Google is empowering

00:49:05.680 --> 00:49:07.210
all sorts of users,
whether you're

00:49:07.210 --> 00:49:13.150
a developer, a backend
engineer, SRE, SQL developer--

00:49:13.150 --> 00:49:15.760
all these kind of
groups and users

00:49:15.760 --> 00:49:18.144
now have the power to bring
machine learning to whatever

00:49:18.144 --> 00:49:19.060
it is that they touch.

00:49:19.060 --> 00:49:19.600
That's pretty awesome.

00:49:19.600 --> 00:49:20.933
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Yeah, exactly.

00:49:20.933 --> 00:49:25.070
So if you think about what's
needed to have machine learning

00:49:25.070 --> 00:49:28.550
be pervasive across
organizations,

00:49:28.550 --> 00:49:30.830
we believe democratization
is what's needed.

00:49:30.830 --> 00:49:33.490
You need to build
tools for people

00:49:33.490 --> 00:49:37.520
and have them use machine
learning where they are.

00:49:37.520 --> 00:49:40.920
You cannot expect that your
developers, to data analysts,

00:49:40.920 --> 00:49:44.510
to data scientists-- all of
them will pick up Python and R.

00:49:44.510 --> 00:49:46.520
And they all will
be a data scientist.

00:49:46.520 --> 00:49:48.200
Rather than doing
that, we believe

00:49:48.200 --> 00:49:50.030
we need to provide tools.

00:49:50.030 --> 00:49:53.340
So anyone can build a
machine learning model.

00:49:53.340 --> 00:49:56.870
And BigQuery ML
specifically targets

00:49:56.870 --> 00:50:00.410
the gap where you have these
data analysts who understand

00:50:00.410 --> 00:50:02.990
your domain, who
understand the data,

00:50:02.990 --> 00:50:05.630
but they do not have the
tools to build a model.

00:50:05.630 --> 00:50:08.300
So we have TensorFlow
for data scientists

00:50:08.300 --> 00:50:10.370
to build a state
of the art model.

00:50:10.370 --> 00:50:13.130
We have AutoML, Cloud ML APIs.

00:50:13.130 --> 00:50:16.760
So developers can just call
an API on standard problems

00:50:16.760 --> 00:50:18.590
like object detection.

00:50:18.590 --> 00:50:22.250
But there's a big gap in terms
of, you have your custom data,

00:50:22.250 --> 00:50:24.110
custom business problems.

00:50:24.110 --> 00:50:27.800
Data analysts are the only
ones who understand it deeply.

00:50:27.800 --> 00:50:30.410
And they can now
start using machine

00:50:30.410 --> 00:50:31.779
learning using BigQuery ML.

00:50:31.779 --> 00:50:33.570
NICK ORLOVE: That's
awesome, very exciting.

00:50:36.420 --> 00:50:39.670
So how does it really
work in the end picture?

00:50:39.670 --> 00:50:41.362
How does that look
for the end users?

00:50:41.362 --> 00:50:42.320
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Yeah.

00:50:42.320 --> 00:50:44.880
So if you think
about an end user,

00:50:44.880 --> 00:50:48.440
the end user can now just
log into the data warehouse

00:50:48.440 --> 00:50:52.710
or go to a notebook or
wherever they are writing SQL.

00:50:52.710 --> 00:50:55.580
Stay in SQL to build a
machine learning model.

00:50:55.580 --> 00:50:59.990
Stay in SQL to train the
model within BigQuery.

00:50:59.990 --> 00:51:02.530
So you actually
bring ML to the data

00:51:02.530 --> 00:51:04.014
rather than taking data to ML.

00:51:04.014 --> 00:51:04.680
NICK ORLOVE: OK.

00:51:04.680 --> 00:51:05.388
That makes sense.

00:51:05.388 --> 00:51:08.120
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: And finally,
I'll show you in a demo,

00:51:08.120 --> 00:51:10.790
just with a couple
lines of SQL, which

00:51:10.790 --> 00:51:13.610
is machine-learning specific,
you can build a model.

00:51:13.610 --> 00:51:17.530
So you do not have to worry
about hyperparameter tuning,

00:51:17.530 --> 00:51:20.270
basic feature
transformations, which

00:51:20.270 --> 00:51:22.180
are needed for almost
every machine learning

00:51:22.180 --> 00:51:23.096
model [? you build. ?]

00:51:23.096 --> 00:51:25.010
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah.

00:51:25.010 --> 00:51:27.260
And I'm curious, what
kind of customers

00:51:27.260 --> 00:51:30.050
are using BigQuery ML?

00:51:30.050 --> 00:51:32.502
And do you have any example
use cases that you can share?

00:51:32.502 --> 00:51:33.710
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Definitely.

00:51:33.710 --> 00:51:38.450
So we usually run a private
alpha before we launch a beta.

00:51:38.450 --> 00:51:40.640
And we had a very
successful alpha.

00:51:40.640 --> 00:51:44.450
These are some of the
public references who

00:51:44.450 --> 00:51:46.740
agreed to share their use case.

00:51:46.740 --> 00:51:48.860
I'll start with
Hearst Newspapers.

00:51:48.860 --> 00:51:54.050
So media companies are usually
spending a lot of effort

00:51:54.050 --> 00:51:57.380
on reducing churn.

00:51:57.380 --> 00:52:02.090
And in their case,
they wanted to start

00:52:02.090 --> 00:52:06.320
doing predictive churn
modeling to do marketing

00:52:06.320 --> 00:52:09.611
campaigns to people who are more
likely to churn, as an example.

00:52:09.611 --> 00:52:10.360
NICK ORLOVE: Sure.

00:52:10.360 --> 00:52:13.160
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: To
do that, they started

00:52:13.160 --> 00:52:15.500
working with BigQuery ML.

00:52:15.500 --> 00:52:17.720
They managed to write their
first churn prediction

00:52:17.720 --> 00:52:18.740
model within a day.

00:52:18.740 --> 00:52:20.010
NICK ORLOVE: That's awesome.

00:52:20.010 --> 00:52:20.640
That's impressive.

00:52:20.640 --> 00:52:22.889
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: And the
model actually combined data

00:52:22.889 --> 00:52:24.420
from six different sources.

00:52:24.420 --> 00:52:26.120
And since this
was SQL, they were

00:52:26.120 --> 00:52:32.750
able to do the join staying
in their SQL platform.

00:52:32.750 --> 00:52:37.140
And post their join, they
just built a model in BQML.

00:52:37.140 --> 00:52:42.590
And then they set up a
pipeline using Cloud Composer

00:52:42.590 --> 00:52:45.650
to do retraining
and do predictions

00:52:45.650 --> 00:52:48.290
and to have a dashboard that
writes the marketing campaigns.

00:52:48.290 --> 00:52:50.750
And they managed to
finish the whole process

00:52:50.750 --> 00:52:53.660
within a couple of
[? scripts. ?] So that's what

00:52:53.660 --> 00:52:55.940
they got by using BigQuery ML.

00:52:55.940 --> 00:52:58.625
20th Century Fox-- they
actually introduced a product,

00:52:58.625 --> 00:53:01.460
in our [INAUDIBLE] demo.

00:53:01.460 --> 00:53:05.720
