01:01:31.340 --> 01:01:32.548
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Definitely.

01:01:32.548 --> 01:01:35.990
So if you think
about it, this model

01:01:35.990 --> 01:01:41.160
did not ask you to set up
an infrastructure to train.

01:01:41.160 --> 01:01:46.340
We leveraged BigQuery, and we
just created as many processes

01:01:46.340 --> 01:01:49.350
as needed to build
this model for you.

01:01:49.350 --> 01:01:52.550
And this is going to work on
a petabyte of training date

01:01:52.550 --> 01:01:53.720
if that's your use case.

01:01:53.720 --> 01:01:56.500
Because you never have to
think about the infrastructure

01:01:56.500 --> 01:02:01.070
in BigQuery to make [INAUDIBLE]
petabyte fairly easily.

01:02:01.070 --> 01:02:04.230
Secondly, there is a
hyperparameter for this called

01:02:04.230 --> 01:02:07.160
learning rate, we auto-tuned it.

01:02:07.160 --> 01:02:09.770
You know that you should
not be overfitting.

01:02:09.770 --> 01:02:12.620
So if your data is large
enough, we automatically

01:02:12.620 --> 01:02:14.400
split it into training and test.

01:02:14.400 --> 01:02:18.770
We do a random 80/20 split,
which is the most common.

01:02:18.770 --> 01:02:21.890
Numeric features should
never be put in as-is.

01:02:21.890 --> 01:02:23.524
So we standardize all of them.

01:02:23.524 --> 01:02:25.190
NICK ORLOVE: And I'm
curious about that.

01:02:25.190 --> 01:02:27.080
Why shouldn't we be
using numeric features

01:02:27.080 --> 01:02:28.819
like dates and stuff like that?

01:02:28.819 --> 01:02:30.610
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: So
with numeric features,

01:02:30.610 --> 01:02:34.100
if their scale is really
different across two features,

01:02:34.100 --> 01:02:36.087
then one starts
dominating over the other.

01:02:36.087 --> 01:02:36.920
NICK ORLOVE: Oh, OK.

01:02:36.920 --> 01:02:38.711
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Let's
say one of features

01:02:38.711 --> 01:02:42.230
is weights and pounds
and one of the features

01:02:42.230 --> 01:02:45.440
ends up being distance,
and the distance is

01:02:45.440 --> 01:02:48.630
up to 2 miles or 2 kilometers.

01:02:48.630 --> 01:02:51.433
Suddenly, your weight will
be a dominant feature.

01:02:51.433 --> 01:02:52.266
NICK ORLOVE: Gotcha.

01:02:52.266 --> 01:02:54.620
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: So you
don't want that to affect.

01:02:54.620 --> 01:02:56.990
Similarly, if one person
builds a model using

01:02:56.990 --> 01:03:00.810
weights in kilograms
and one in pounds,

01:03:00.810 --> 01:03:02.580
your model will start
looking different.

01:03:02.580 --> 01:03:02.850
NICK ORLOVE: OK.

01:03:02.850 --> 01:03:03.440
That makes sense.

01:03:03.440 --> 01:03:05.273
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: So
you want to standardize

01:03:05.273 --> 01:03:06.360
your numeric features.

01:03:06.360 --> 01:03:08.490
And models work with numbers.

01:03:08.490 --> 01:03:12.110
So let's say you have string
features like categories

01:03:12.110 --> 01:03:14.880
or Booleans.

01:03:14.880 --> 01:03:18.960
So categories you cannot
just feed in X, Y, Z.

01:03:18.960 --> 01:03:21.390
We automatically won't hard
encode them to convert them

01:03:21.390 --> 01:03:22.930
to numbers.

01:03:22.930 --> 01:03:24.540
So these are some
of the basic things

01:03:24.540 --> 01:03:32.680
that we do which enable getting
to such a good model so fast.

01:03:32.680 --> 01:03:33.480
NICK ORLOVE: Cool.

01:03:33.480 --> 01:03:35.630
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: And if
you're an advanced user--

01:03:35.630 --> 01:03:37.590
you know more machine learning--

01:03:37.590 --> 01:03:40.790
you can use L1/L2
regularization.

01:03:40.790 --> 01:03:44.040
You can select how to split
your training and test data.

01:03:44.040 --> 01:03:46.430
So if you wanted a
date-based split,

01:03:46.430 --> 01:03:49.910
or if you wanted a
different split in 80/20,

01:03:49.910 --> 01:03:52.420
you can specify all those.

01:03:52.420 --> 01:03:55.640
And finally, you can set your
own learning rate as well.

01:03:55.640 --> 01:03:59.330
The philosophy that
we have is everything

01:03:59.330 --> 01:04:03.920
that gets to a better model
is going to be automated.

01:04:03.920 --> 01:04:09.185
And things that allow the power
users to get to a better model,

01:04:09.185 --> 01:04:11.412
we'll provide them as options.

01:04:11.412 --> 01:04:12.620
And that's what we are doing.

01:04:12.620 --> 01:04:15.560
So you can always just
write two lines of SQL

01:04:15.560 --> 01:04:17.070
and get to a really good model.

01:04:17.070 --> 01:04:18.320
NICK ORLOVE: That's fantastic.

01:04:21.390 --> 01:04:24.780
And so we've obviously
showed how simple it can be.

01:04:24.780 --> 01:04:26.790
But how about some
of the more complex

01:04:26.790 --> 01:04:29.790
features, some of the more
complex aspects of the machine

01:04:29.790 --> 01:04:30.600
learning?

01:04:30.600 --> 01:04:32.720
What do we support
from that perspective?

01:04:32.720 --> 01:04:37.230
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: So we
support creation and prediction

01:04:37.230 --> 01:04:39.240
using the model
from Standard SQL.

01:04:39.240 --> 01:04:41.550
You can use UDFs with them.

01:04:41.550 --> 01:04:45.320
We are starting with two types
of machine learning models.

01:04:45.320 --> 01:04:47.350
We'll add more over time.

01:04:47.350 --> 01:04:49.890
But to evaluate
how the model did,

01:04:49.890 --> 01:04:52.360
we actually have
evaluation functions

01:04:52.360 --> 01:04:57.360
which give you ROC curve, or
fixed logistic regression,

01:04:57.360 --> 01:05:00.240
specific stats for
linear regression.

01:05:00.240 --> 01:05:03.240
We allow you to
inspect the weights

01:05:03.240 --> 01:05:07.470
to see how much a feature
impacted the prediction.

01:05:07.470 --> 01:05:09.900
And finally, we have
table-valued functions

01:05:09.900 --> 01:05:13.200
so you can also analyze
your features to see

01:05:13.200 --> 01:05:14.715
how they were distributed.

01:05:14.715 --> 01:05:17.490
And that's something
that's very useful

01:05:17.490 --> 01:05:18.830
doing feature engineering.

01:05:18.830 --> 01:05:19.580
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah.

01:05:19.580 --> 01:05:21.121
Yeah, I know that
can be a little bit

01:05:21.121 --> 01:05:24.037
complex and involved,
especially from TensorFlow side.

01:05:24.037 --> 01:05:25.120
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Exactly.

01:05:25.120 --> 01:05:27.703
NICK ORLOVE: So it seems like
we simplify this, but still give

01:05:27.703 --> 01:05:30.170
you the ability to kind of
do some tuning and all that.

01:05:30.170 --> 01:05:30.360
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Yes.

01:05:30.360 --> 01:05:30.720
NICK ORLOVE: That's cool.

01:05:30.720 --> 01:05:32.011
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Yes, exactly.

01:05:32.011 --> 01:05:33.660
And let's see if
the model finished.

01:05:33.660 --> 01:05:35.584
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah.

01:05:35.584 --> 01:05:37.000
ABHISHEK KASHYAP:
So it did finish

01:05:37.000 --> 01:05:39.470
in 3 minutes and 12 seconds.

01:05:39.470 --> 01:05:45.730
And if we just go to
the model, what you see

01:05:45.730 --> 01:05:47.500
is some basic model
details-- when

01:05:47.500 --> 01:05:52.270
they were built, what type it
is, what the last type was.

01:05:52.270 --> 01:05:54.450
We use a creative
gradient [? design. ?]

01:05:54.450 --> 01:06:00.400
So it shows you what different
parameters were chosen.

01:06:00.400 --> 01:06:02.200
And if you look at
the stats, it will

01:06:02.200 --> 01:06:04.600
show you the last stats
for training and evaluation

01:06:04.600 --> 01:06:09.280
data, and the learning rate,
and how much time it took.

01:06:09.280 --> 01:06:11.500
Model schema shows you
the different fields

01:06:11.500 --> 01:06:15.100
we used as features
and their types.

01:06:15.100 --> 01:06:18.010
So these are the ones we
had in the SELECT statement.

01:06:18.010 --> 01:06:20.290
And we are going to
add more charts here,

01:06:20.290 --> 01:06:23.135
so you can start looking
at curves, and precision

01:06:23.135 --> 01:06:24.560
recall, et cetera.

01:06:24.560 --> 01:06:25.550
NICK ORLOVE: Oh, awesome,
within the UI itself?

01:06:25.550 --> 01:06:27.174
ABHISHEK KASHYAP:
Within the UI itself.

01:06:27.174 --> 01:06:29.802
NICK ORLOVE: Cool, that's
going to be useful.

01:06:29.802 --> 01:06:31.670
ABHISHEK KASHYAP:
And for now, you

01:06:31.670 --> 01:06:37.470
can actually look
at it in Data Studio

01:06:37.470 --> 01:06:38.670
directly off of BigQuery.

01:06:38.670 --> 01:06:41.561
NICK ORLOVE: Oh, OK.

01:06:41.561 --> 01:06:45.267
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: So we got a
model built on 22 gigs of data.

01:06:45.267 --> 01:06:46.600
NICK ORLOVE: Took three minutes.

01:06:46.600 --> 01:06:47.000
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Three minutes.

01:06:47.000 --> 01:06:47.680
NICK ORLOVE: Two lines of code.

01:06:47.680 --> 01:06:48.560
ABHISHEK KASHYAP:
No machine learning.

01:06:48.560 --> 01:06:49.020
NICK ORLOVE: That's powerful.

01:06:49.020 --> 01:06:51.520
Yeah, no machine learning
experience required.

01:06:51.520 --> 01:06:53.140
Cool.

01:06:53.140 --> 01:06:55.480
And I think one thing
that we kind of touched on

01:06:55.480 --> 01:06:58.240
that we should
emphasize is, BigQuery

01:06:58.240 --> 01:07:01.960
is one piece of the entire
Google Cloud data pipeline

01:07:01.960 --> 01:07:02.520
strategy.

01:07:02.520 --> 01:07:04.960
So before data
gets into BigQuery,

01:07:04.960 --> 01:07:07.422
you can perform ETL,
maybe through Dataflow.

01:07:07.422 --> 01:07:08.630
You mentioned Cloud Composer.

01:07:08.630 --> 01:07:14.230
So you can compose how things
are being run in terms of jobs,

01:07:14.230 --> 01:07:16.960
and when jobs start,
when they end, et cetera.

01:07:16.960 --> 01:07:21.640
And then finally, the data
that's being stored in BigQuery

01:07:21.640 --> 01:07:23.360
is typically in a
tabular format--

01:07:23.360 --> 01:07:25.300
so lots of rows,
lots of columns.

01:07:25.300 --> 01:07:26.740
And a normal human
being might not

01:07:26.740 --> 01:07:29.620
be quite as good at
actually plotting those data

01:07:29.620 --> 01:07:31.370
points out if you
have thousands,

01:07:31.370 --> 01:07:33.630
hundreds of thousands
of rows, who knows?

01:07:33.630 --> 01:07:37.120
And so the amazing thing
is that BigQuery is already

01:07:37.120 --> 01:07:39.880
integrated into quite a
few business intelligence

01:07:39.880 --> 01:07:40.550
dashboards.

01:07:40.550 --> 01:07:42.730
So this data can
already be plugged

01:07:42.730 --> 01:07:47.830
into sources like Data Studio,
Tableau, Looker, et cetera.

01:07:47.830 --> 01:07:50.140
In fact, one of the
really awesome use cases

01:07:50.140 --> 01:07:51.910
that I recently
read is from Tableau

01:07:51.910 --> 01:07:55.960
where they took some of
the public data that's

01:07:55.960 --> 01:07:58.300
out there on home
prices in Seattle.

01:07:58.300 --> 01:08:02.470
And they ran a BigQuery machine
learning model against it

01:08:02.470 --> 01:08:05.950
and identified what
would a home cost

01:08:05.950 --> 01:08:08.410
if you want two bedrooms,
three bedrooms, if you

01:08:08.410 --> 01:08:12.369
want that fancy waterfront
view, or in town, square footage

01:08:12.369 --> 01:08:12.910
of the house.

01:08:12.910 --> 01:08:14.680
I mean, it's pretty
transformational.

01:08:14.680 --> 01:08:18.460
And it's exciting that you
can build something that's

01:08:18.460 --> 01:08:22.060
quite comprehensive from just
a couple of lines of code,

01:08:22.060 --> 01:08:23.470
spending a couple of minutes.

01:08:23.470 --> 01:08:26.410
It's pretty exciting,
so that's really cool.

01:08:26.410 --> 01:08:27.430
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:08:27.430 --> 01:08:28.388
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Yeah.

01:08:28.388 --> 01:08:30.609
So as a user, if you log
in to their dashboard,

01:08:30.609 --> 01:08:33.220
you can manually
type in these fields.

01:08:33.220 --> 01:08:35.301
And it will use BigQuery
ML in the background

01:08:35.301 --> 01:08:36.759
to get to you the
predicted prices.

01:08:36.759 --> 01:08:37.852
NICK ORLOVE: Cool.

01:08:37.852 --> 01:08:39.939
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: So
it's fairly interactive.

01:08:39.939 --> 01:08:43.279
And that's what you need to
use a BI platform for anyways.

01:08:43.279 --> 01:08:45.069
NICK ORLOVE: Yes,
exactly, exactly--

01:08:45.069 --> 01:08:48.220
how to drill down from the
massive amounts of data

01:08:48.220 --> 01:08:50.060
into something a little
bit more meaningful.

01:08:50.060 --> 01:08:50.830
Cool.

01:08:50.830 --> 01:08:54.100
And I also hear that Looker
has some specific integrations

01:08:54.100 --> 01:08:55.330
with BigQuery ML.

01:08:55.330 --> 01:08:56.830
So maybe you can
touch on those too?

01:08:56.830 --> 01:08:58.288
ABHISHEK KASHYAP:
Yeah, definitely.

01:08:58.288 --> 01:09:03.359
So Looker, we have a deep
partnership with where

01:09:03.359 --> 01:09:07.000
they have integrated BigQuery
ML to their markup language,

01:09:07.000 --> 01:09:08.359
called LookML.

01:09:08.359 --> 01:09:12.340
And you can actually create
a model string within Looker

01:09:12.340 --> 01:09:15.074
without actually having to
go and write SQL BigQuery.

01:09:15.074 --> 01:09:16.990
NICK ORLOVE: OK, without
getting into BigQuery

01:09:16.990 --> 01:09:17.529
and running a SQL statement?

01:09:17.529 --> 01:09:17.930
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Exactly, yes.

01:09:17.930 --> 01:09:18.350
NICK ORLOVE: Cool.

01:09:18.350 --> 01:09:20.990
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: And on top
of that, some of the evaluation

01:09:20.990 --> 01:09:22.399
charts I showed--

01:09:22.399 --> 01:09:24.689
they built some
templates for those--

01:09:24.689 --> 01:09:27.370
what they call Looker Blocks.

01:09:27.370 --> 01:09:30.550
And they have operationalized
the whole ML workflow.

01:09:30.550 --> 01:09:32.890
So Looker customers
can actually start

01:09:32.890 --> 01:09:37.529
using BigQuery ML through their
native integration [INAUDIBLE]..

01:09:37.529 --> 01:09:40.974
And that where we are headed
with other BI platforms

01:09:40.974 --> 01:09:41.850
as well.

01:09:41.850 --> 01:09:42.850
NICK ORLOVE: Cool, nice.

01:09:42.850 --> 01:09:44.308
Yeah, it's awesome
that everyone is

01:09:44.308 --> 01:09:45.934
kind of adopting
this as a feature

01:09:45.934 --> 01:09:47.850
and pushing it out to
all their customers too.

01:09:47.850 --> 01:09:48.790
ABHISHEK KASHYAP:
Yes, because everyone

01:09:48.790 --> 01:09:50.874
realizes that machine
learning is the future.

01:09:50.874 --> 01:09:52.430
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah, I think so.

01:09:52.430 --> 01:09:54.320
ABHISHEK KASHYAP:
And we actually

01:09:54.320 --> 01:09:56.020
have talked to a lot
of data analysts.

01:09:56.020 --> 01:09:58.180
And they all are taking
courses on Coursera.

01:09:58.180 --> 01:10:01.210
And they're all reading up.

01:10:01.210 --> 01:10:03.310
And we believe the more
tools we can provide

01:10:03.310 --> 01:10:06.620
to make the transition
easier, the better it is,

01:10:06.620 --> 01:10:10.090
and the better the proliferation
of machine learning

01:10:10.090 --> 01:10:14.930
will be across the enterprises.

01:10:14.930 --> 01:10:19.340
And if you just think about your
industry, and think about some

01:10:19.340 --> 01:10:24.970
of the business problems,
there are actually

01:10:24.970 --> 01:10:27.820
a lot of them which could
be machine learning-based.

01:10:27.820 --> 01:10:32.230
And what we have seen is, if
you take retail, and if you say,

01:10:32.230 --> 01:10:35.590
I can optimize inventory
in a predictive way,

01:10:35.590 --> 01:10:37.210
I can predict
which item is going

01:10:37.210 --> 01:10:41.630
to be needed in which
region in what quantity.

01:10:41.630 --> 01:10:45.916
That actually, you would think
it's only an efficiency play.

01:10:45.916 --> 01:10:47.290
But more than
that, it's actually

01:10:47.290 --> 01:10:49.271
a customer satisfaction
play as well.

01:10:49.271 --> 01:10:50.270
NICK ORLOVE: OK, how so?

01:10:50.270 --> 01:10:52.103
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Because
now your customers

01:10:52.103 --> 01:10:54.400
in a particular
region are not going

01:10:54.400 --> 01:10:56.860
to see longer shipping times.

01:10:56.860 --> 01:10:59.470
They're not going
to see stockouts.

01:10:59.470 --> 01:11:03.790
So there are lot of operational
supply chain, marketing,

01:11:03.790 --> 01:11:08.680
forecasting problems where
just drawing a straight line

01:11:08.680 --> 01:11:13.100
from a historical trend is
actually fairly inefficient.

01:11:13.100 --> 01:11:16.060
So if you could use machine
learning for each of those--

01:11:16.060 --> 01:11:18.550
and some of our users are
actually working with that data

01:11:18.550 --> 01:11:20.200
day-in, day-out--

01:11:20.200 --> 01:11:23.110
then that's going to have a
great impact on the business

01:11:23.110 --> 01:11:25.804
metrics themselves.

01:11:25.804 --> 01:11:27.350
NICK ORLOVE: Cool.

01:11:27.350 --> 01:11:29.660
So I think it's important
to also touch on--

01:11:29.660 --> 01:11:32.780
since this is a brand new
component of BigQuery--

01:11:32.780 --> 01:11:34.920
how to start getting
started with it,

01:11:34.920 --> 01:11:36.300
how to get your hands on it.

01:11:36.300 --> 01:11:39.400
The exciting thing is that
BigQuery ML is available today

01:11:39.400 --> 01:11:40.060
in beta.

01:11:40.060 --> 01:11:42.680
And so you can start
using it today,

01:11:42.680 --> 01:11:44.060
start building your own models.

01:11:44.060 --> 01:11:46.520
Some great resources
for getting trained--

01:11:46.520 --> 01:11:49.040
obviously, watching
this webinar helps,

01:11:49.040 --> 01:11:51.290
but it doesn't get
you completely there.

01:11:51.290 --> 01:11:53.660
So there are some
fantastic blogs

01:11:53.660 --> 01:11:56.210
that are out there,
a couple particularly

01:11:56.210 --> 01:11:58.760
from a Googler named Lak.

01:11:58.760 --> 01:12:01.910
He has written some
fantastic blogs about how

01:12:01.910 --> 01:12:03.710
to predict flight times.

01:12:03.710 --> 01:12:06.810
I think there's another
one about predicting

01:12:06.810 --> 01:12:09.180
natality datasets, which
are really awesome.

01:12:09.180 --> 01:12:13.340
So if you Google search
Lak, BigQuery ML,

01:12:13.340 --> 01:12:15.170
you'll find probably
like three or four

01:12:15.170 --> 01:12:17.370
blogs on that specific subject.

01:12:17.370 --> 01:12:19.320
So I'd definitely
give that a read.

01:12:19.320 --> 01:12:22.790
Secondly, there
are courses already

01:12:22.790 --> 01:12:26.720
out there on BigQuery ML
and actually breaks down

01:12:26.720 --> 01:12:31.209
some of the necessary components
around BigQuery, big data,

01:12:31.209 --> 01:12:33.500
machine learning, the concepts
behind machine learning.

01:12:33.500 --> 01:12:36.140
Because while we've made it
pretty simple for our users,

01:12:36.140 --> 01:12:37.640
it's still important
to understand

01:12:37.640 --> 01:12:40.610
how the machine learning
actually works in the backend,

01:12:40.610 --> 01:12:44.190
the different types of
algorithms, stuff like that.

01:12:44.190 --> 01:12:48.530
And there's already a course
on that on coursera.com.

01:12:48.530 --> 01:12:50.570
So there are data
insights on course,

01:12:50.570 --> 01:12:51.810
which is pretty phenomenal.

01:12:51.810 --> 01:12:53.540
I've gone through that myself.

01:12:53.540 --> 01:12:55.700
And then obviously, you
start getting your hands

01:12:55.700 --> 01:12:57.890
on BigQuery ML, start using it.

01:12:57.890 --> 01:13:00.200
And if there's any
feedback that you have,

01:13:00.200 --> 01:13:04.580
go ahead and flood this
email address here,

01:13:04.580 --> 01:13:07.375
