BigQuery to use BigQuery ML?"

01:15:59.800 --> 01:16:02.850
I'm thinking, like, federated
queries, that kind of aspect.

01:16:02.850 --> 01:16:06.130
Does the data actually have to
be in BigQuery ML or BigQuery?

01:16:06.130 --> 01:16:07.440
Or can it be external as well?

01:16:07.440 --> 01:16:09.100
ABHISHEK KASHYAP:
Yeah, so are right.

01:16:09.100 --> 01:16:12.250
We can federate from GCS today.

01:16:12.250 --> 01:16:14.980
So your data could be
in GCS or BigQuery.

01:16:14.980 --> 01:16:19.040
The whole idea of BigQuery ML is
that you bring ML to the data.

01:16:19.040 --> 01:16:22.190
And you leverage the
BigQuery's processing power.

01:16:22.190 --> 01:16:27.480
So your data, for this to
work, has to be in BigQuery.

01:16:27.480 --> 01:16:29.411
Or if it's in GCS,
we can federate.

01:16:29.411 --> 01:16:30.286
NICK ORLOVE: Awesome.

01:16:30.286 --> 01:16:31.720
ABHISHEK KASHYAP:
And we can just

01:16:31.720 --> 01:16:33.861
get it in and use BigQuery ML

01:16:33.861 --> 01:16:35.860
NICK ORLOVE: Man, that
makes it pretty powerful.

01:16:35.860 --> 01:16:39.070
Because you can query across
multiple sources of data,

01:16:39.070 --> 01:16:42.460
therefore, kind of
eliminating that aspect

01:16:42.460 --> 01:16:43.797
of having different data silos.

01:16:43.797 --> 01:16:44.880
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Exactly.

01:16:44.880 --> 01:16:46.110
NICK ORLOVE: Cool, cool.

01:16:46.110 --> 01:16:48.340
OK, onto the second question.

01:16:48.340 --> 01:16:51.840
"How can we use BigQuery
ML on unstructured data?"

01:16:51.840 --> 01:16:54.640
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: It depends
on the unstructured data,

01:16:54.640 --> 01:16:55.930
what the data is.

01:16:55.930 --> 01:16:59.855
If you think about
unstructured as short strings,

01:16:59.855 --> 01:17:04.030
say category of a
user, et cetera,

01:17:04.030 --> 01:17:07.510
we won't hard-encode them,
and you can use it directly.

01:17:07.510 --> 01:17:10.260
If you start thinking
unstructured data as, say,

01:17:10.260 --> 01:17:15.190
pages and paragraphs and
phrases, in that case,

01:17:15.190 --> 01:17:18.250
that data needs to be converted
to a number for BigQuery ML

01:17:18.250 --> 01:17:19.710
to use.

01:17:19.710 --> 01:17:22.270
And to convert to that
number, the typical process

01:17:22.270 --> 01:17:25.860
is to convert it into
some kind of an embedding.

01:17:25.860 --> 01:17:29.080
Like an example could
be, you have a document,

01:17:29.080 --> 01:17:32.880
and you use Word2vec to
convert it to a vector

01:17:32.880 --> 01:17:36.400
in embedding that
knows which phrases

01:17:36.400 --> 01:17:38.980
are similar to which phrases.

01:17:38.980 --> 01:17:40.810
And then you can take
those two points,

01:17:40.810 --> 01:17:43.480
and you know that closer
points are similar.

01:17:43.480 --> 01:17:47.210
And then you can feed those
into BigQuery ML as a feature.

01:17:47.210 --> 01:17:51.220
So to work with unstructured
data that's a lot of text,

01:17:51.220 --> 01:17:53.770
you'll have to convert
them into some embedding.

01:17:53.770 --> 01:17:56.440
And the embedding could be
a traditional NLP technique

01:17:56.440 --> 01:18:00.030
like Bag-of-Words, or TF-IDF.

01:18:00.030 --> 01:18:02.050
Or it could be
something very advanced,

01:18:02.050 --> 01:18:04.010
like Word2vec, Doc2vec.

01:18:04.010 --> 01:18:08.590
Or you could also call
AutoML API, or Cloud ML API,

01:18:08.590 --> 01:18:12.280
convert it into some
structured semantics

01:18:12.280 --> 01:18:17.440
like, which are the entities,
and what's the sentiment,

01:18:17.440 --> 01:18:20.910
and use those in
BigQuery ML as-is.

01:18:20.910 --> 01:18:22.910
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah, that's
an excellent point.

01:18:22.910 --> 01:18:28.300
Because BigQuery ML is not the
only machine learning platform

01:18:28.300 --> 01:18:28.940
within Google.

01:18:28.940 --> 01:18:31.840
So if you want to send
some unstructured images,

01:18:31.840 --> 01:18:35.500
send them to the Vision API,
videos to the Intelligence API.

01:18:35.500 --> 01:18:38.470
And you can get structured
responses, like you said,

01:18:38.470 --> 01:18:40.210
sentiment, maybe
identify the nouns,

01:18:40.210 --> 01:18:44.020
the verbs, the
content of images.

01:18:44.020 --> 01:18:46.210
And you can place those
in your BigQuery dataset,

01:18:46.210 --> 01:18:47.777
and then query them
with BigQuery ML.

01:18:47.777 --> 01:18:48.860
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Exactly.

01:18:48.860 --> 01:18:49.630
NICK ORLOVE: That's cool.

01:18:49.630 --> 01:18:51.645
That kind of ties everything
together, that's awesome.

01:18:51.645 --> 01:18:53.950
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Yes, and
as you think about some

01:18:53.950 --> 01:18:56.050
of the more advanced
applications--

01:18:56.050 --> 01:18:59.020
like if I want to do
stock predictions better,

01:18:59.020 --> 01:19:01.705
you should also get some
news into the model, right?

01:19:01.705 --> 01:19:02.580
NICK ORLOVE: Ah, yes.

01:19:02.580 --> 01:19:04.038
ABHISHEK KASHYAP:
Suddenly, you can

01:19:04.038 --> 01:19:07.525
start seeing, once you go past
just using structured data,

01:19:07.525 --> 01:19:10.610
how easily you can start getting
unstructured data to improve

01:19:10.610 --> 01:19:11.110
your model.

01:19:11.110 --> 01:19:11.860
NICK ORLOVE: Cool.

01:19:11.860 --> 01:19:12.550
Awesome.

01:19:12.550 --> 01:19:13.160
Very cool.

01:19:13.160 --> 01:19:13.660
OK.

01:19:13.660 --> 01:19:15.520
So jumping to the
third question.

01:19:15.520 --> 01:19:18.130
"How do I retrain the
model with new data?"

01:19:18.130 --> 01:19:18.880
That's a good one.

01:19:18.880 --> 01:19:20.020
ABHISHEK KASHYAP:
And that's actually

01:19:20.020 --> 01:19:21.561
how you would put
it into production.

01:19:21.561 --> 01:19:25.090
So what we see is,
depending on your use case,

01:19:25.090 --> 01:19:30.000
depending on how quickly
your inherent data changes,

01:19:30.000 --> 01:19:32.200
customers retrain it
anywhere from once

01:19:32.200 --> 01:19:34.390
a day to once a quarter.

01:19:34.390 --> 01:19:38.200
So you have to decide what
the right frequency is.

01:19:38.200 --> 01:19:43.390
And since this is entirely in
SQL, through the BigQuery API,

01:19:43.390 --> 01:19:45.880
you would use a
scheduling platform.

01:19:45.880 --> 01:19:48.310
Like we have Composer,
which is based

01:19:48.310 --> 01:19:50.320
on open source [INAUDIBLE].

01:19:50.320 --> 01:19:54.410
That could just call
your Create Model query,

01:19:54.410 --> 01:19:58.300
point it to the new data, which
could be written as a view,

01:19:58.300 --> 01:20:01.979
and just retrain it at the
frequency that you chose.

01:20:01.979 --> 01:20:03.520
And then it waits
for the model to be

01:20:03.520 --> 01:20:08.660
built since you are calling
it from the Composer API.

01:20:08.660 --> 01:20:12.194
And once it's built, you start
using it for productions.

01:20:12.194 --> 01:20:12.860
NICK ORLOVE: OK.

01:20:12.860 --> 01:20:13.901
That seems pretty simple.

01:20:13.901 --> 01:20:15.700
Cool, OK.

01:20:15.700 --> 01:20:18.400
Number four, "Can I
export a BigQuery ML

01:20:18.400 --> 01:20:21.400
model for online predictions?"

01:20:21.400 --> 01:20:22.680
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Yes.

01:20:22.680 --> 01:20:29.280
So exporting as a function is
not provided out of the gate.

01:20:29.280 --> 01:20:33.720
But we do provide
ML.WEIGHTS, which you

01:20:33.720 --> 01:20:35.860
can use for feature inspection.

01:20:35.860 --> 01:20:38.980
So you can take the
weights of the model,

01:20:38.980 --> 01:20:42.820
and you can export it to
your online infrastructure

01:20:42.820 --> 01:20:45.520
to use the model that way.

01:20:45.520 --> 01:20:49.905
So ML.WEIGHTS is the way to
export a model today and use it

01:20:49.905 --> 01:20:51.300
for online predictions.

01:20:51.300 --> 01:20:52.350
And talking about that--

01:20:52.350 --> 01:20:55.740
Lak actually wrote a blog
on this particular problem

01:20:55.740 --> 01:20:56.666
as well.

01:20:56.666 --> 01:20:58.290
And in that blog, he
walks through step

01:20:58.290 --> 01:20:59.880
by step on how you
would set it up.

01:20:59.880 --> 01:21:02.160
NICK ORLOVE: OK, cool.

01:21:02.160 --> 01:21:05.550
And then final question, "How
does BigQuery ML pricing work?

01:21:05.550 --> 01:21:09.444
And I'm thinking in terms of the
slot usage and other aspects.

01:21:09.444 --> 01:21:10.360
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: Yes.

01:21:10.360 --> 01:21:14.440
So BigQuery ML-- we have
preview pricing right now,

01:21:14.440 --> 01:21:15.870
since it's in beta.

01:21:15.870 --> 01:21:18.610
And BigQuery has two types
of pricing structure.

01:21:18.610 --> 01:21:24.030
One is flat rate, where you buy
a set number of slots, which

01:21:24.030 --> 01:21:26.430
are processing
units, and on-demand,

01:21:26.430 --> 01:21:29.880
where you pay for the
data that you query.

01:21:29.880 --> 01:21:34.560
So for flat rate, during
the preview pricing,

01:21:34.560 --> 01:21:37.740
we are going to include
it with the current slots.

01:21:37.740 --> 01:21:40.630
So if you are
already on flat rate,

01:21:40.630 --> 01:21:42.255
you use the same
slots for BigQuery ML.

01:21:42.255 --> 01:21:43.020
NICK ORLOVE: OK, makes sense.

01:21:43.020 --> 01:21:45.020
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: And
then you can mix and match

01:21:45.020 --> 01:21:47.940
your other jobs
with ML jobs to get

01:21:47.940 --> 01:21:49.680
to a really good utilization.

01:21:49.680 --> 01:21:53.010
For on-demand, a
prediction or evaluation

01:21:53.010 --> 01:21:54.750
is like a standard query.

01:21:54.750 --> 01:21:57.060
So that's $5 at terabyte.

01:21:57.060 --> 01:22:00.490
For model creation,
during the preview,

01:22:00.490 --> 01:22:03.510
we are charging $5 a gigabyte.

01:22:03.510 --> 01:22:07.650
And the reason is, one, even
though it's two lines of SQL,

01:22:07.650 --> 01:22:09.970
it's actually a lot
of work underneath

01:22:09.970 --> 01:22:12.060
to get to a really good model.

01:22:12.060 --> 01:22:14.110
And secondly, it's
taking away a good six

01:22:14.110 --> 01:22:16.425
to eight hours of
infrastructure set up.

01:22:16.425 --> 01:22:17.040
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah,
that also makes sense.

01:22:17.040 --> 01:22:19.050
ABHISHEK KASHYAP: And that's
the value of this is providing.

01:22:19.050 --> 01:22:19.800
NICK ORLOVE: Yeah.

01:22:19.800 --> 01:22:21.593
OK, that makes perfect sense.

01:22:21.593 --> 01:22:22.540
Cool.

01:22:22.540 --> 01:22:23.520
Well, fantastic.

01:22:23.520 --> 01:22:27.040
I'm glad you were able to get
through all those questions.

01:22:27.040 --> 01:22:29.290
So Abhishek, thank you
so much for joining us.

01:22:29.290 --> 01:22:31.289
I'm really thrilled
to have you here.

01:22:31.289 --> 01:22:32.830
And for all of our
viewers out there,

01:22:32.830 --> 01:22:35.080
thank you for joining the
session on the BigQuery ML.

